---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import QuickAnswer from '../../components/QuickAnswer.astro';
import FaqBlock from '../../components/FaqBlock.astro';
import RelatedLinks from '../../components/RelatedLinks.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../consts';

const path = '/guides/lock-snapping-explained';
const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What is lock snapping?',
    a: 'Lock snapping is a forced-entry method where a euro cylinder is broken at its weakest point so the lock mechanism can be operated. The Master Locksmiths Association says it can take 5–10 seconds on a vulnerable cylinder and needs no specialist tools.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which doors are at risk of lock snapping?',
    a: 'Any door locked by a euro-profile cylinder — most uPVC and composite doors, and many flat entrance doors — especially where the cylinder is unrated or sticks out past the handle. On a multipoint door every locking point is driven by the cylinder, so the cylinder is the weak point.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I stop my lock being snapped?',
    a: 'Fit a cylinder rated TS007 3-star (or Sold Secure SS312 Diamond), sized so it sits flush with the handle, or protect a 1-star cylinder with a 2-star security handle or guard. A 3-star cylinder is the simpler option because it does not rely on the handle.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is lock snapping still common?',
    a: 'Figures the Master Locksmiths Association obtained from West Yorkshire Police showed snapping used in 15.87% of burglaries at its 2011 peak and 8.32% in 2019. That is one police force, and there is no current national figure, but police forces still advise upgrading to 3-star cylinders.',
  },
];
---
<ContentLayout
  path={path}
  lead="How the attack works, what the evidence actually shows, and the one change that stops it on most London doors."
  sources={[
    { label: 'Master Locksmiths Association — Lock snapping', url: 'https://www.locksmiths.co.uk/faq/lock-snapping/' },
    { label: 'Master Locksmiths Association — Lock snapping burglary statistics (West Yorkshire Police FOI)', url: 'https://www.locksmiths.co.uk/lock-snapping-burglary-statistics/' },
    { label: 'West Yorkshire Police — Euro cylinder advice', url: 'https://www.westyorkshire.police.uk/advice/home-security/home-security/euro-cylinder-advice' },
    { label: 'Avocet Group — ABS Master (snap mechanism)', url: 'https://avocetgroup.co.uk/products/abs-master-3-star-cylinder' },
  ]}
>
  <QuickAnswer>
    <p>
      Lock snapping breaks a euro cylinder at its weak point so the lock can be turned — the Master Locksmiths
      Association says it can take <strong>5–10 seconds</strong> with no specialist tools. It targets uPVC, composite
      and flat doors with unrated cylinders. The fix is a <strong>TS007 3-star</strong> (or Sold Secure Diamond)
      cylinder sized to sit flush with the handle.
    </p>
  </QuickAnswer>

  <h2>How the attack works</h2>
  <p>
    A euro cylinder is a single brass body with a cam in the middle that drives the lock. On most uPVC and
    composite doors, that cam operates the whole multipoint locking strip. If the cylinder sticks out past the
    handle, it can be gripped and forced until it breaks at its thinnest point, exposing the cam. Once the cam
    is turned, every locking point on the door opens with it.
  </p>

  <h2>What the evidence says</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The MLA obtained West Yorkshire Police data showing snapping in <strong>15.87%</strong> of burglaries at the 2011 peak, falling to <strong>8.32%</strong> by 2019, as better cylinders spread.</li>
    <li>Attempted snapping in the same data fell from 6.04% to 2.67%.</li>
    <li>This is <strong>one force’s data</strong>, and we have not found a current national or London-wide figure. Treat it as evidence that snapping was — and remains — a real method, not as a London statistic.</li>
    <li>West Yorkshire Police notes that “some offenders have developed a way of overcoming” older anti-snap designs, which is why tested ratings matter more than “anti-snap” labels.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How anti-snap cylinders resist it</h2>
  <p>
    A 3-star cylinder is tested against snapping as part of TS007. Designs differ: some use a sacrificial front
    section that breaks away while the core stays intact; Avocet describes a cam that locks down inside a hardened
    core when the ABS is attacked, so the door stays locked. What matters to you is the tested rating on the
    cylinder face — see <a href="/guides/ts007-3-star-explained/">TS007 3-star explained</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What to do now</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Check the cylinder face for three stars (or one star plus a 2-star handle).</li>
    <li>Check the cylinder doesn’t stick out past the handle by more than a few millimetres.</li>
    <li>Don’t forget back and side doors — they often have the cheapest cylinder in the house.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    We replace euro cylinders with an Avocet ABS 3-star for <strong>{SITE.price} flat</strong> per door
    across North &amp; Central London and Hertfordshire. <a href="/3-star-lock-upgrade-london/">Book an upgrade</a>.
  </p>

  <FaqBlock faqs={faqs} heading="Lock snapping questions" bare />

  <RelatedLinks links={[
    { href: '/guides/best-anti-snap-euro-cylinders-uk/', title: 'Anti-snap cylinders compared', blurb: 'The 3-star, Diamond options.' },
    { href: '/3-star-lock-upgrade-london/', title: 'Upgrade to 3-star', blurb: `${SITE.price} fitted.` },
    { href: '/emergency-locksmith-london/', title: 'Snapped lock? 24/7', blurb: 'Emergency callout, quoted per job.' },
  ]} />
</ContentLayout>
